arm64: leave architected timer controls untouched under the Harmony clock

The Harmony clockevent runs through MMIO and the virtual timer PPI. Its
shutdown path still read and wrote CNTV_CTL_EL0 just to clear ENABLE, while
the compiled physical timer handler and shutdown path retained CNTP_CTL_EL0
accesses even though the DT requires the virtual PPI. These instructions
prevent the executable-image audit from banning timer control accesses. An
admitted control write can enable KVM's physical timer, whose state is not in
the snapshot: a cold restore then changes the guest's next CNTP_CTL_EL0 read.

Under the Harmony clock, disarm only the MMIO clockevent. Fail if the unused
physical handler or shutdown path is reached. The timer-control scanner can
then reject all physical and virtual control instructions in the image.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a diff under the Harmony
repository's designated guest-kernel patch exception.

---
--- a/drivers/clocksource/arm_arch_timer.c
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/arm_arch_timer.c
@@ -596,6 +596,9 @@
 static irqreturn_t arch_timer_handler_phys(int irq, void *dev_id)
 {
 	struct clock_event_device *evt = dev_id;
+
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HARMONY_ARM_PVCLOCK))
+		panic("Harmony physical timer IRQ cannot be registered");
 
 	return timer_handler(ARCH_TIMER_PHYS_ACCESS, evt);
 }
@@ -615,8 +618,6 @@
 static int arch_timer_shutdown_virt(struct clock_event_device *clk)
 {
 	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HARMONY_ARM_PVCLOCK)) {
-		/* Keep KVM's live-domain virtual timer disabled for the VM's life. */
-		arch_timer_shutdown(ARCH_TIMER_VIRT_ACCESS, clk);
 		harmony_arm_clockevent_disarm();
 		return 0;
 	}
@@ -626,6 +627,9 @@
 
 static int arch_timer_shutdown_phys(struct clock_event_device *clk)
 {
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HARMONY_ARM_PVCLOCK))
+		panic("Harmony physical timer cannot be selected");
+
 	return arch_timer_shutdown(ARCH_TIMER_PHYS_ACCESS, clk);
 }
 
